<div class="bg-surface-base min-h-screen text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-neutral-50 mb-4">
      Chain reorganizations
    </h1>
    <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed">
      A reorganization happens when two miners find a block at the same height and the network briefly holds two versions of the chain's tip.
      Every node follows the branch with the most work, so when the other branch pulls ahead, nodes switch to it and the blocks they had become stale.
      Their transactions are not lost: most were also mined on the winning branch, and the rest return to the mempool to be mined again.
    </p>
    <p
      id="reorg-what-happens"
      class="leading-relaxed"
    >
      Most reorganizations are one block deep and settle when the next block arrives, and the deeper a block sits under later blocks, the less likely it is to change.
      That is why a payment is treated as more final with each confirmation, and why an explorer or index follows its node to the winning branch rather than keeping the stale one.
    </p>

    <figure
      id="reorg-drawing"
      class="my-10"
    >
      <img
        src={~p"/images/ink-wash/reorg-fork.webp"}
        alt="A row of wooden blocks on one thread splits in two: six grey-blue blocks carry on, two orange blocks stop with the last tipped over, and an hourglass stands by the fork."
        width="1200"
        height="800"
        loading="lazy"
        class="w-full h-auto rounded-lg bg-white"
      />
      <figcaption class="mt-3 text-sm text-gray-500 dark:text-gray-400">
        Two branches race from one block.
        The one with more work carries on, and the other stops.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section
      id="reorg-math"
      aria-labelledby="reorg-math-heading"
    >
      <h2
        id="reorg-math-heading"
        class="text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-50 mb-4"
      >
        The math, from the whitepaper
      </h2>
      <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        The
        <a
          href="https://bitcoin.org/bitcoin.pdf"
          class="underline"
          rel="noopener"
        >Bitcoin whitepaper</a>
        sets this out itself.
        Section 5 describes the everyday case: two nodes broadcast different blocks at the same height, each works on the one it saw first and keeps the other, and "the tie will be broken when the next proof-of-work is found and one branch becomes longer."
        Section 11, Calculations, puts a number on how fast a block settles.
      </p>
      <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Call the honest network's share of the hashpower <em>p</em> and an attacker's <em>q</em>.
        The race between them is the Gambler's Ruin problem: an attacker <em>z</em> blocks behind ever catches up with chance
        <span class="font-mono">(q/p)<sup>z</sup></span>
        when <em>p</em> is larger, and certainly when it is not.
        While the recipient waits for <em>z</em> blocks, the attacker's own progress follows a Poisson distribution with
        <span class="font-mono">λ = z·q/p</span>,
        so the chance the attacker ever overtakes is
      </p>
      <p class="my-4 break-words font-mono text-sm text-neutral-50">
        P = 1 − Σ<sub>k=0..z</sub> (λ<sup>k</sup> e<sup>−λ</sup> / k!) · (1 − (q/p)<sup>z−k</sup>)
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-neutral-50 mt-8 mb-3">
        A worked example
      </h3>
      <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Take an attacker with 10% of the hashpower (<em>q</em> = 0.1, <em>p</em> = 0.9) and a payment one block deep (<em>z</em> = 1), so λ = 0.111.
      </p>
      <ul class="mb-3 list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 leading-relaxed">
        <li>
          The attacker has found no block yet (k = 0): chance e<sup>−0.111</sup> = 0.895.
          They still have one block to make up, which they manage one time in nine, so this case adds 0.895 × (1 − 0.111) = 0.795.
        </li>
        <li>
          The attacker has already found one (k = 1): chance 0.099.
          Nothing is left to make up, so this case adds nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
          P = 1 − 0.795 ≈ 0.205, the paper's own figure (0.2045873).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mb-3 leading-relaxed">
        Each further block shrinks the chance:
      </p>
      <table
        id="reorg-math-table"
        class="w-full my-4 text-sm"
      >
        <thead class="text-left text-neutral-50">
          <tr>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">Attacker's share</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">1 block deep</th>
            <th class="py-2 pr-4 font-semibold">6 blocks deep</th>
            <th class="py-2 font-semibold">Depth for under 0.1%</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-gray-700 font-mono tabular-nums">
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">10%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">20.5%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">0.024%</td>
            <td class="py-2">5 blocks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">30%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">62.8%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">13.2%</td>
            <td class="py-2">24 blocks</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">45%</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">—</td>
            <td class="py-2 pr-4">—</td>
            <td class="py-2">340 blocks</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="leading-relaxed">
        The paper models a deliberate attacker.
        Most reorganizations are the Section 5 kind, an honest tie that the next block settles.
        The same math is why each confirmation counts for more than the last.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</div>
